  "readmeFile": "Readme.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Current changes\n\n- Adds a netstandard2.1 sender asset alongside the existing net8.0 and net10.0 assets.\n- Preserves the public sink API, HTTP acknowledgment rules and persisted spool format across targets; no receiver change is required.\n- Uses Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 8.0.34 across target frameworks.\n- Limits new effective ApplicationId values to 255 ASCII characters. Longer normalized IDs retain their first 189 characters in a reserved representation with the complete normalized ID's lowercase SHA-256 hash.\n- Limits configured and entry-assembly ApplicationVersion values to 255 UTF-16 code units without splitting surrogate pairs.\n- Keeps historical spool metadata unchanged. Existing 256-character IDs now resolve to a different effective identity and default spool directory; coordinate upgrades with receivers enforcing the new limit.\n\n0.1.0\n\nInitial public SerilogRelay package.\n\n- Targets .NET 8 and .NET 10.\n- Adds the Serilog `.WriteTo.SerilogRelay(...)` sink for HTTP log delivery.\n- Uses one configuration method with optional grouped SerilogRelayOptions for delivery, retention, and other relay settings.\n- Requires a completed HTTP 204 No Content response to acknowledge a batch; other statuses remain eligible for retry, and redirects follow the HTTP handler's policy.\n- Limits built-in HTTP response bodies to 4 KiB and retains valid non-204 Retry-After hints when body reading fails; supplied HTTP clients keep their own size limits.\n- Persists events to a bounded local SQLite application spool before normal network delivery.\n- Reuses stable `EventId` values across retries and restarts while using `BatchId` for delivery-attempt correlation.\n- Supports shared application spools across multiple processes with atomic short-lived delivery claims and takeover after process failure.\n- Allows surviving or newer processes to drain backlog created by another process of the same application.\n- Provides configurable sent retention, optional unsent maximum age, and physical spool-size limits.\n- Adds process-local exponential endpoint retry with jitter and optional HTTP `Retry-After` handling on every non-204 response, capped by EndpointRetry.MaximumDelay (five minutes by default).\n- Deletes successfully acknowledged spool rows immediately by default; sent retention remains an explicit opt-in.\n- Uses the complete spool schema for capacity probes and rolls back reclaimed rows when replacement fails.\n- Adds a bounded emergency memory buffer for persistence failures and capacity rejection, evicting the oldest queued events when necessary.\n- Adds configurable shutdown delivery: a three-second total budget, one-second retries after failures, and immediate successive sends after success. SQLite cleanup can finish after the application wait ends.\n- Adds SQLite corruption quarantine/recovery and recreates the spool when corruption is detected.\n- Uses normal platform TLS certificate validation by default; accepting any server certificate requires an explicit dangerous opt-in.\n- Supports an optional opaque bearer token sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.\n- Includes `ApplicationId`, `MachineId`, and `ProcessId` producer metadata in the relay payload; normalized ApplicationId values are limited to 256 characters.\n- Uses at-least-once HTTP delivery semantics: non-204/transport failures remain pending for later retry or takeover.\n- Keeps receiver persistence, duplicate presentation, and server-side storage policy outside the sink contract.",
